 {:db/id "3d41bbd8-0239-5e0b-bc1a-94b15180f9ac"
  :question/id #uuid "3d41bbd8-0239-5e0b-bc1a-94b15180f9ac"
  :question/problem "Source question: Canvas Question 4 (question_59459022).\n\nThe standard basis for $\\mathbb R^n$ has 6 vectors in it."
  :question/answer-fields ["2522467c-4ab6-52b1-88eb-e406d5e6b8f7"]
  :question/worked-solution "The standard basis is $\\{\\mathbf e_1,\\ldots,\\mathbf e_n\\}$, where $\\mathbf e_j$ has a $1$ in coordinate $j$ and zeros elsewhere. There is one vector per coordinate, hence $n$ vectors. For example, $\\mathbb R^2$ has standard basis $\\{(1,0),(0,1)\\}$ with two vectors. The source statement holds in the special case $n=6$, but is false as a general assertion about $\\mathbb R^n$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "a94acfef-ccad-57c1-ac43-3b4a7bb68f2b"
