 {:db/id "a5e7faae-c11f-5729-aa65-abf94f90a3b4"
  :question/id #uuid "a5e7faae-c11f-5729-aa65-abf94f90a3b4"
  :question/problem "Source question: Canvas Question 6 (question_59459024).\n\nThe dimension of a subspace $V$ of $\\mathbb R^n$ is the largest number of nonzero linearly independent vectors that are contained in $V$."
  :question/answer-fields ["f582f6a1-1f2b-54c9-8ac2-d774a50d19c0"]
  :question/worked-solution "Let $d=\\dim V$ and choose a basis $\\{\\mathbf b_1,\\ldots,\\mathbf b_d\\}$. These $d$ vectors form an independent subset of $V$.\n\nTo check that a larger independent subset is impossible, express any $m>d$ vectors of $V$ in this basis. Put their coordinate columns into a $d\\times m$ matrix $C$. The homogeneous system $C\\mathbf a=\\mathbf0$ has more unknowns than rows, so it has a free variable and a nonzero solution. The same coefficients give a nontrivial zero combination of the original vectors; therefore they are dependent.\n\nThus the largest possible independent-set size is $d$, the number of vectors in a basis for $V$. The word **nonzero** is redundant, since any set containing the zero vector is dependent. For $V=\\{\\mathbf0\\}$, the empty basis has zero vectors and $\\dim V=0$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
